---
/**
 * The front door: what to do, what it is worth, and what the rest of the site is for.
 *
 * Three jobs, in this order. Tell the reader what to do and roughly what it pays before
 * they have scrolled. Give them the rule that stops every number here being misread,
 * which is that a saving against your own old portfolio and an edge against an index
 * fund answer different questions and may never be added. Then hand them onward.
 *
 * Provenance lives in one block at the foot rather than in a note beside every
 * paragraph, and every `<Figure>` already carries its own source, interval and date.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Do the certain things first"
  bareTitle
  description="Cut your fees, use whatever tax-advantaged accounts you have, and hold each fund where it is taxed least. Worth about 1.09% a year against the portfolio you already own, and you would know inside four months."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Do the certain things first</h1>

    <p>Cut your fees. Use whatever tax-advantaged accounts you actually have. Hold each fund in the account that
    taxes it least.</p>

    <p>Against the portfolio a normal person would otherwise be holding, those three moves are worth about
    <Figure id="structural-budget" inline />. On a hundred thousand dollars that is roughly a thousand dollars a
    year, for an afternoon of paperwork. They come out of fund contracts and tax statute rather than out of a
    forecast, so nobody has to be right about markets for them to pay. And you would know inside about
    <Figure id="structural-budget-horizon" inline /> whether you got them right.</p>

    <p>Everything else on this site is a bet of some size. This part is arithmetic.</p>

    <p>If you already own one cheap index fund in one account and never touch it, your number is close to zero and
    we have nothing to sell you. Most people sit between the two, which is why the honest outer bound on the
    package runs <Figure id="structural-budget-range" inline />.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout heading="Three yardsticks, never added" class="mt-10">
    <div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <Figure id="structural-budget" />
      <Figure id="edge-vs-cheap-index" />
      <Figure id="behaviour-gap" />
    </div>
  </Breakout>

  <Callout kind="do-this">
    <p>
      Open your plan statement and find one number: what the fund holding most of your money charges each year.
      Weighted by the money in them, index funds and active funds charge
      <Figure id="index-vs-active-fee" inline />. If yours sits near the top of that pair, you have just found the
      largest reliable gain available to you today.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>Better than what, exactly</h2>

    <p>Any claim about a portfolio has to finish the sentence "better than what". Three endings make sense, and
    they are three separate questions with three unrelated answers.</p>

    <p>Against the portfolio you already hold, the answer is the number above, and it settles fast because only
    <Figure id="own-portfolio-tracking-error" inline /> of wander sits around it. Against a cheap total-market
    index fund, it is <Figure id="edge-vs-cheap-index" inline />, which is about five dollars a year on ten
    thousand and too small for us to tell from zero. Against what a typical fund investor earns after buying late
    and selling into falls, Morningstar's ten-year gap is <Figure id="behaviour-gap" inline />.</p>

    <p>Those three never add. Adding them is the most common way a result here has turned out wrong, and we know
    because we did it to ourselves. An old headline of ours for the account-placement decision summed lines
    measured against three different yardsticks and booked an avoided cost as though it were a saving. The
    defensible figure, against something a real investor could build, is <Figure id="placement-value" inline />.
    The research code now raises an error rather than adding across yardsticks.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>What counts as a bet, and how big</h2>

    <p>Here is the part that costs us something to write.</p>

    <p>Add up everything this project has measured that might beat a plain cheap index fund, keep only what
    survives its own error bars, and what is left is <Figure id="edge-vs-cheap-index" inline />. The wander around
    it is <Figure id="index-tracking-error" inline />. In a normal year you would land within about three
    percentage points either side of the index, for no reason except that you own different things. Call it sixty
    times as much noise as signal. Over thirty years your chance of finishing ahead of the index is
    <Figure id="odds-ahead-of-cheap-index" inline />.</p>

    <p>So it is a coin flip. We can't tell you this beats a cheap index fund.</p>

    <p>That does not mean a cheap index fund cannot be beaten. It means we cannot show you that we can beat one,
    on the data anyone has. A site telling you otherwise is either measuring against something easier or has not
    checked how long its own claim would take to prove.</p>

    <p>Four months against thirty-one years, on the same rule applied twice. That gap is the whole reason to take
    the fees and the tax before you take a view on anything.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="caveat" label="What would change our mind">
    <p>
      A fund shelf that cut its published edge below a fifth of a percent a year, or a decade in which the funds
      leaning toward cheap and profitable companies trailed the index by more than their stated wander, would turn
      that coin flip into a rejection.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>Where to go from here</h2>

    <p><a href="/start/">The checklist</a> puts the whole thing in order, and every step says up front who it is
    for. <a href="/tools/placement/">The placement tool</a> runs the account-ordering arithmetic at your own tax
    rates and your own account sizes, which is the only form of that answer worth acting on.</p>

    <p><a href="/portfolio/">The portfolio</a> is one concrete construction, with the parts that are bets named as
    bets. <a href="/stacking/">Stacking</a> covers why owning more good ideas stops helping sooner than you would
    think, and why how you pay for an addition matters more than what you add.
    <a href="/doesnt-work/">What doesn't work</a> covers market timing, crypto as ballast, crash insurance and
    direct indexing, each turned down on a measurement and each with the number that would reopen it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>Where this comes from</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Standing caveats">
      <p>
        The author holds no licence, works for no firm and belongs to no professional body. AI was used heavily
        throughout. Backtests are hindsight with a chart attached.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Fee, tax and account figures are read from fund filings and tax statute rather than from a backtest,
    checked on <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>. The comparison against a cheap index fund comes from
    <a href="/research/expected-edge-decomposition/">where outperformance can come from</a>, and the wander in it
    is assumed rather than measured, so every probability here is a best case.
    <a href="/how-sure/">How sure we are</a> lists every error this project has caught in itself.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
